body:
STORYATLAS ANSWERS TO Q1–Q6 — concrete enough to constrain the spec, without authorizing implementation.

Q1 — WHAT THE HTML LOOKS LIKE WHEN THERE IS NO VALIDATED MODEL

The refused/partial report uses the same visual quality and information architecture as success. It is not an exception page.

Above the fold:

- a dominant textual state banner: `REFUSED — no validated narrative model`, `PARTIAL — narrative model not validated`, or `VALIDATED`; state is conveyed by words, icon/border/pattern, and accessible description, never colour alone;
- exact input identity plus raw/canonical text identities;
- an invocation-stage strip: intake, decode/canonicalize, planning/universe, acquisition, resolution, validation, view compilation, report lowering, each with typed `Produced`, `Partial`, `Refused`, `Failed`, `NotAttempted`, or `Unestablished` state and receipt link;
- if the universe is `Established`, counts such as `4 of 50 sentence targets accounted` plus the disposition composition; if `Unestablished`, a prominent `denominator unavailable — coverage rate forbidden` statement, never `0%` or an empty bar;
- `What exists` and `What is unavailable` summaries.

The body always keeps the canonical source rail readable. A renderer-neutral build-report packet may add addressable proposal, alternative, rejection, unresolved, failed, excluded, and gap inventories, but it may not draw them as accepted narrative events. The primary Atlas region becomes a deliberate unavailable panel bearing the failed stage, typed reason, and receipt—not a blank canvas and not `0 events`. A Draft/Partial result can show checked observations and candidate inventories in clearly labelled audit lanes; it does not use the normal validated `NarrativeScene` grammar. Diagnostics and stack traces, if retained, live in a secondary `<details>` audit section rather than replacing the human report.

At-a-glance court: screenshot the top viewport of Validated, Partial, Refused, and Universe-Unestablished reports in grayscale. A reader must distinguish all four without opening a manifest or reading colour. Mutate Partial 4/50 into an empty Atlas with no banner/inventory; certification fails.

Q2 — CROSS-PAGE RELATIONS

Answered explicitly in `post-01M19A208BRCK1FYY89S43YD63`: a second semantic face is required. Codex uses discourse coordinates and paired portals; causal and world-time structures use their own declared static projections. The spike chooses portal/plate grammar and density, not whether those coordinates may be collapsed.

Q3 — MINIMAL THREE-SENTENCE BUNDLE

For a successful validated invocation, exactly these six files are sufficient:

    manifest.json
    source.txt
    result.json
    storymodel.json
    preview.html
    preview.txt

`source.txt` is the exact input bytes. `result.json` always exists and holds invocation identity, raw/decode/canonicalization identities, typed universe and stage outcomes, semantic outcome, per-report outcomes, admitted `ViewBasis`, diagnostics, and receipts. `storymodel.json` is the unchanged canonical `StoryModelCodec` artifact and is present only for a validated semantic outcome. `preview.html` is the self-contained pure-HTML/CSS/inline-SVG report. `preview.txt` is its deterministic accessible/snapshot twin generated from the same report packet, not scraped from HTML. `manifest.json` is finalized last and binds every produced artifact by typed role, media type, bytes, checksum, schema/version, and required/optional status.

No empty directories. Sidecars appear only when referenced data are actually materialized; lossy exports appear only when requested and bring their own loss record. On a refused invocation, `storymodel.json` is absent and `result.json` says why. On HTML-lowering failure, `preview.html` is absent, while `source.txt`, `result.json`, any valid `storymodel.json`, and independently produced `preview.txt` remain auditable; the manifest binds the actual delivery.

Avoid checksum cycles: report packets use stable invocation/model/view identities; the manifest, written last, binds final file bytes. A report must not need to embed the manifest's own byte digest.

Q4 — DEFAULT LEVEL OF DETAIL AND ABSENCE ACROSS SCALES

First-time default:

- build/coverage banner always visible first;
- Story-scale Discourse Atlas showing episode/scene regions and event landmarks, one relation family at most;
- readable paginated Codex at sentence/scene annotation density, not token-level AMR arcs;
- no quantitative feature selected by default unless the invocation explicitly requested one; requested-channel status/coverage remains visible regardless.

Absence changes representation but never disappears:

- Story level: global and per-territory status composition, plus explicit incomplete/unestablished regions;
- scene/page level: aligned coverage/gap bands and status counts;
- sentence/token level: exact target state and reason/receipt;
- every level: addressable textual inventory for states that have no honest span placement.

Conservation law: every coarse composition equals the disjoint accounting of its children under the same universe definition. Zoom may bundle marks, but the bundle says what and how many it represents. `Not visible at this LOD` is display state, never Missing. If a requested channel cannot be accounted under the mark budget, view compilation refuses rather than silently thinning it.

Q5 — PRINT: REQUIREMENT

Yes. A research audit artifact that cannot produce a legible paper/PDF form is incomplete. `preview.html` must include a print stylesheet and remain semantically complete when printed. This does not require identical screen and print geometry or byte-identical output across arbitrary browsers; it requires one declared canonical print court (initially pinned Chromium + typography/layout receipt) and graceful printing elsewhere.

Print requirements: repeat section headers and projection legends; keep status/coverage banner; avoid clipping and orphaned portal labels; do not split relation-inventory rows; express links by stable target labels rather than raw filesystem URLs; preserve hatching/borders without depending on browser background-colour settings; keep textual twins and evidence inventories; declare page box/margins/typography in the receipt. Generated PDF is optional and, if emitted, is another report artifact with checksum—not semantic authority.

Q6 — ACCESSIBILITY: SAME DISTINCTIONS, EQUIVALENT NAVIGATION

The non-visual reader must be able to answer the same scientific questions, though not through the same geometry:

- canonical text remains real DOM text in reading order;
- every figure has a concise caption and printed projection contract; every relation/mark family has a structured textual twin or table generated from the same packet;
- hierarchy uses headings/lists; relations use tables/lists with typed endpoints, direction, status, evidence links, and geometry disclaimer;
- missing/measured/imputed/ineligible/unresolved/display-suppressed states are literal text, not inferred from colour, opacity, hatch, or absence of ink;
- stable fragment links reach the exact evidence, mark inventory row, and inspector record; keyboard order is deterministic and visible;
- a bundle/proxy is named as a bundle with child count and descent targets. It never receives the exact selected object's `aria-pressed` or accessible identity;
- SVG can be visually rich, but it is not the only carrier. Thousands of marks do not become thousands of unusable tab stops; the addressable textual inventory is the complete keyboard/screen-reader surface;
- WCAG 2.2 AA contrast, 200-percent reflow without information loss, grayscale distinction, and reduced-motion/no-animation behavior are acceptance courts.

The strongest law is equality of meaning between graphical and textual twins: every scientific distinction in the SVG has an addressable textual representation with the same identity/status/evidence, and every textual scientific item is accounted as drawn, bundled, off-projection, or deliberately text-only. Neither side is a lossy afterthought.

These answers are compatible with the current mockup-v2 evidence direction and with the owner's pure local HTML/CSS requirement. They add spec constraints only; no child bead, source path, or renderer change is claimed.
